# PART 7 — EVENTS API 2.0 (SERVER-SIDE TRACKING READINESS)

Design the tracking architecture to support TikTok Events API 2.0 in addition to browser-side Pixel tracking.

This must be implemented securely and conditionally.

## 7.1 Browser Pixel

Implement the browser Pixel as the active first tracking layer.

## 7.2 Server-Side Events API

If the existing project environment has a suitable backend:

- Prepare a secure server-side Events API 2.0 integration.
- Use TikTok's current official endpoint and payload schema.
- Store the Events API access token as a server-side secret.
- Never expose the access token in frontend JavaScript.
- Never use a VITE_ variable for a private server token.
- Never invent credentials.
- Provide configuration documentation for the token.

If there is no backend or no access token available:

- Do not pretend server-side tracking is active.
- Do not build a fake API integration.
- Keep the browser Pixel working.
- Document the exact prerequisites for activating Events API 2.0.

## 7.3 Event Deduplication

If the same conversion is sent through both the Browser Pixel and Events API:

Implement event deduplication.

Requirements:

- Generate one unique event_id for each real action.
- Use the same event_id for the browser and server copies of that same action.
- Use the same event name.
- Do not reuse the same event_id for unrelated actions.
- Follow TikTok's current deduplication requirements.
- Verify the deduplication status through Events Manager.

Do not enable server-side duplicate events without proper event_id handling.

